Chapter: MqttEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter Mqtt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The MQTT v5 client endpoint: the binding that turns the Mqtt codec --
 which only ever built packets, and until 2026-07-23 could not read one
 at all -- into a session with a broker.

 It is PURE, in the style of `CoapEndpoint`, `TlsEndpoint` and
 `DtlsEndpoint`: bytes in, bytes out, no socket and no clock. The
 transport `now` is a caller-supplied tick that drives retransmission and
 keepalive, and a thin caller carries the bytes over TCP. The reliability
 and correlation live here and are exercised by a loopback; the wire is
 `tools/mqtt-client.codex`.

 WHY THIS IS NOT SHAPED LIKE THE CoAP ONE. CoAP runs on datagrams, so its
 endpoint takes exactly one datagram and answers with whole datagrams.
 MQTT runs on TCP, which delivers a byte STREAM: a segment can carry half
 a packet, or three packets, or the tail of one and the head of the next.
 So this endpoint takes a buffer and reports how much of it it consumed,
 and the caller keeps the remainder. Getting that wrong is not a subtle
 failure -- it desynchronises the stream permanently on the first
 coalesced segment, which is exactly what a broker sends under load.

 QoS 1 IS THE RELIABILITY, AND TCP IS NOT IT. TCP guarantees the bytes
 arrive at the far socket; it says nothing about the broker having
 accepted the message, and a reconnect loses whatever was in flight. So a
 QoS 1 PUBLISH is held until its PUBACK matches by packet identifier, and
 retransmitted with the DUP flag if the broker stays silent past the
 deadline. That is the retransmission this row wanted, at the layer that
 can actually promise anything.

 AN MqttEp IS THREADED LINEARLY, like every other endpoint here. The
 state updates go through `__record-set`, which stores into the record and
 returns the same one, so a value that has been passed to one of these
 functions is spent and the caller must use what came back. Branching two
 exchanges off one endpoint value does not give two sessions -- it gives
 one session and two names for it, and the older name then reads state it
 does not own.

 To mqtt-ep-connect gives MqttEpStep, opening the session.
 To mqtt-ep-recv gives MqttEpStep, feeding whatever bytes have arrived.
 To mqtt-ep-tick gives MqttEpStep, advancing the retransmission and keepalive clocks.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  mqtt-t-connack : Integer = 2
  mqtt-t-publish : Integer = 3
  mqtt-t-puback : Integer = 4
  mqtt-t-pubrec : Integer = 5
  mqtt-t-pubrel : Integer = 6
  mqtt-t-pubcomp : Integer = 7
  mqtt-t-suback : Integer = 9
  mqtt-t-pingresp : Integer = 13

  mqtt-st-idle : Integer = 0
  mqtt-st-connecting : Integer = 1
  mqtt-st-ready : Integer = 2
  mqtt-st-failed : Integer = 3

 Where an outstanding publish has got to. QoS 1 has one step and QoS 2
 has two, and the difference matters on the retransmission path: an
 unanswered PUBLISH is resent, but an unanswered PUBREL is resent as a
 PUBREL. Resending the PUBLISH there would re-run an exchange the broker
 has already taken delivery of, which is precisely the duplicate "exactly
 once" exists to prevent.

  mqtt-ph-none : Integer = 0
  mqtt-ph-puback : Integer = 1
  mqtt-ph-pubrec : Integer = 2
  mqtt-ph-pubcomp : Integer = 3

 The republish deadline, in the same unit as the caller's `now`. MQTT
 fixes no value for this -- section 4.4 says only that a client MUST
 redeliver an unacknowledged message -- so it is a choice, and a
 conservative one: long enough that an ordinary broker answers first.

  mqtt-ack-timeout : Integer = 5000
  mqtt-max-retransmit : Integer = 4

Section: Types

  MqttEp = record {
    me-state : Integer,
    me-next-id : Integer,
    me-connack : Integer,
    me-suback : Integer,
    me-sub-id : Integer,
    me-pending-id : Integer,
    me-pending-packet : List Integer,
    me-pending-deadline : Integer,
    me-retries : Integer,
    me-acked : Boolean,
    me-phase : Integer,
    me-in-topic : List Integer,
    me-in-payload : List Integer,
    me-in-count : Integer,
    me-in-rec-id : Integer
  }

  MqttEpStep = record {
    ms-ep : MqttEp,
    ms-out : List (List Integer),
    ms-consumed : Integer
  }

Section: Construction

  mqtt-ep-new : MqttEp
  mqtt-ep-new =
    MqttEp { me-state = mqtt-st-idle, me-next-id = 1, me-connack = (-1), me-suback = (-1),
             me-sub-id = (-1), me-pending-id = (-1), me-pending-packet = [], me-pending-deadline = 0,
             me-retries = 0, me-acked = True, me-phase = mqtt-ph-none,
             me-in-topic = [], me-in-payload = [], me-in-count = 0, me-in-rec-id = (-1) }

  mqtt-ep-idle : MqttEp -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-idle (ep) = MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep, ms-out = [], ms-consumed = 0 }

 A packet identifier is drawn from a counter that wraps at 65535 and
 never yields zero, which the protocol forbids (section 2.2.1).

  mqtt-id-inc : Integer -> Integer
  mqtt-id-inc (n) = if n >= 65535 then 1 else n + 1

Section: Opening The Session

  mqtt-ep-connect : MqttEp, MqttConnectConfig -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-connect (ep) (cfg) =
    MqttEpStep { ms-ep = __record-set ep "me-state" mqtt-st-connecting,
                 ms-out = [mqtt-build-connect cfg], ms-consumed = 0 }

  mqtt-ep-subscribe : MqttEp, Text, MqttQos -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-subscribe (ep) (topic) (qos) =
    if ep.me-state /= mqtt-st-ready then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else let pid = ep.me-next-id
    in let ep2 = __record-set (__record-set ep "me-next-id" (mqtt-id-inc pid)) "me-sub-id" pid
    in MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep2, ms-out = [mqtt-build-subscribe pid topic qos], ms-consumed = 0 }

Section: Publishing

 At QoS 0 the packet goes out and is forgotten -- there is nothing to
 wait for and nothing to retransmit, which is what "at most once" means.
 At QoS 1 it is held: the identifier, the exact bytes, and the deadline,
 because a message that cannot be resent is a message with no delivery
 guarantee at all.

 ONE OUTSTANDING PUBLISH AT A TIME. A second while one is unacknowledged
 is refused rather than queued, the same call `CoapEndpoint` makes for the
 same reason: a queue is a larger client's concern and this one says so by
 not pretending to have one.

  mqtt-ep-publish : MqttEp, Text, List Integer, MqttQos, Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-publish (ep) (topic) (payload) (qos) (now) =
    if ep.me-state /= mqtt-st-ready then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if ep.me-acked == False then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if mqtt-qos-value qos == 0
    then MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep, ms-out = [mqtt-build-publish topic payload qos False 0], ms-consumed = 0 }
    else let pid = ep.me-next-id
    in let pkt = mqtt-build-publish topic payload qos False pid
    in let phase = if mqtt-qos-value qos == 1 then mqtt-ph-puback else mqtt-ph-pubrec
    in let a1 = __record-set ep "me-next-id" (mqtt-id-inc pid)
    in let a2 = __record-set a1 "me-pending-id" pid
    in let a3 = __record-set a2 "me-pending-packet" pkt
    in let a4 = __record-set a3 "me-pending-deadline" (now + mqtt-ack-timeout)
    in let a5 = __record-set a4 "me-retries" 0
    in let a6 = __record-set a5 "me-acked" False
    in let ep2 = __record-set a6 "me-phase" phase
    in MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep2, ms-out = [pkt], ms-consumed = 0 }

Section: Receiving

 Take as many whole packets out of the buffer as it holds, and report the
 byte count consumed so the caller can keep the rest. A partial packet at
 the end consumes nothing and waits for more bytes.

  mqtt-ep-recv : MqttEp, List Integer, Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-recv (ep) (buf) (now) = mqtt-ep-drain ep buf 0 [] now

  mqtt-ep-drain : MqttEp, List Integer, Integer, List (List Integer), Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-drain (ep) (buf) (used) (outs) (now) =
    when mqtt-parse (mqtt-slice buf used (list-length buf))
      is None -> MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep, ms-out = outs, ms-consumed = used }
      is Just (p) ->
        let step = mqtt-ep-on-packet ep p now
        in mqtt-ep-drain (step.ms-ep) buf (used + p.mp-total) (outs & (step.ms-out)) now

  mqtt-ep-on-packet : MqttEp, MqttPacket, Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-packet (ep) (p) (now) =
    if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-connack then mqtt-ep-on-connack ep p
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-suback then mqtt-ep-on-suback ep p
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-puback then mqtt-ep-on-puback ep p
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-pubrec then mqtt-ep-on-pubrec ep p now
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-pubcomp then mqtt-ep-on-pubcomp ep p
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-pubrel then mqtt-ep-on-pubrel ep p
    else if p.mp-type == mqtt-t-publish then mqtt-ep-on-publish ep p
    else mqtt-ep-idle ep

 A non-zero CONNACK reason is the broker refusing, and the session is
 failed rather than quietly left half-open. Reason 135 is "not
 authorized", which is what an unauthenticated client gets from a broker
 that requires credentials -- the case the dead username field made
 unreachable.

  mqtt-ep-on-connack : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-connack (ep) (p) =
    let reason = mqtt-connack-reason p
    in let ep2 = __record-set ep "me-connack" reason
    in mqtt-ep-idle (__record-set ep2 "me-state" (if reason == 0 then mqtt-st-ready else mqtt-st-failed))

 A SUBACK is matched by packet identifier. One that names a subscription
 we did not send is not our confirmation and is dropped -- without the
 check, any SUBACK at all would confirm any subscription.

  mqtt-ep-on-suback : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-suback (ep) (p) =
    if mqtt-suback-id p /= ep.me-sub-id then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else mqtt-ep-idle (__record-set ep "me-suback" (mqtt-suback-reason p))

 A PUBACK clears the outstanding publish, and only if the identifier
 matches. A stale acknowledgement from an earlier message must not retire
 the current one.

  mqtt-ep-on-puback : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-puback (ep) (p) =
    if ep.me-phase /= mqtt-ph-puback then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if mqtt-ack-id p /= ep.me-pending-id then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else mqtt-ep-idle (mqtt-ep-retire ep)

  mqtt-ep-retire : MqttEp -> MqttEp
  mqtt-ep-retire (ep) =
    let r1 = __record-set ep "me-acked" True
    in let r2 = __record-set r1 "me-pending-id" (-1)
    in let r3 = __record-set r2 "me-pending-packet" []
    in __record-set r3 "me-phase" mqtt-ph-none

 QoS 2, publishing half. PUBREC says the broker has taken delivery and is
 holding it; PUBREL releases it and PUBCOMP retires the exchange. The
 pending packet becomes the PUBREL, because that is now the message whose
 loss must be recovered from -- keeping the PUBLISH there is what makes a
 timeout redeliver something the broker already has.

  mqtt-ep-on-pubrec : MqttEp, MqttPacket, Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-pubrec (ep) (p) (now) =
    if ep.me-phase /= mqtt-ph-pubrec then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if mqtt-ack-id p /= ep.me-pending-id then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else let rel = mqtt-build-pubrel (ep.me-pending-id)
    in let q1 = __record-set ep "me-pending-packet" rel
    in let q2 = __record-set q1 "me-phase" mqtt-ph-pubcomp
    in let q3 = __record-set q2 "me-pending-deadline" (now + mqtt-ack-timeout)
    in let ep2 = __record-set q3 "me-retries" 0
    in MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep2, ms-out = [rel], ms-consumed = 0 }

  mqtt-ep-on-pubcomp : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-pubcomp (ep) (p) =
    if ep.me-phase /= mqtt-ph-pubcomp then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if mqtt-ack-id p /= ep.me-pending-id then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else mqtt-ep-idle (mqtt-ep-retire ep)

 QoS 2, receiving half. The broker releases a message it had held; we
 answer PUBCOMP for the identifier we acknowledged with PUBREC. A PUBREL
 naming an identifier we never recorded is answered anyway -- section
 4.3.3 requires it, because that is exactly what a PUBCOMP lost in
 transit looks like from the other end, and refusing would strand the
 broker retrying forever.

  mqtt-ep-on-pubrel : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-pubrel (ep) (p) =
    let id = mqtt-ack-id p
    in MqttEpStep { ms-ep = __record-set ep "me-in-rec-id" (-1), ms-out = [mqtt-build-pubcomp id], ms-consumed = 0 }

 An inbound PUBLISH is the delivery a subscription exists for. At QoS 1
 it is acknowledged; unacknowledged, a broker redelivers it forever, which
 is a livelock wearing the costume of reliability.

  mqtt-ep-on-publish : MqttEp, MqttPacket -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-on-publish (ep) (p) =
    let b1 = __record-set ep "me-in-topic" (mqtt-publish-topic p)
    in let b2 = __record-set b1 "me-in-payload" (mqtt-publish-payload p)
    in let ep2 = __record-set b2 "me-in-count" ((ep.me-in-count) + 1)
    in if mqtt-publish-qos p == 1
    then MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep2, ms-out = [mqtt-build-puback (mqtt-publish-id p)], ms-consumed = 0 }
    else if mqtt-publish-qos p == 2
    then MqttEpStep { ms-ep = __record-set ep2 "me-in-rec-id" (mqtt-publish-id p),
                      ms-out = [mqtt-build-pubrec (mqtt-publish-id p)], ms-consumed = 0 }
    else mqtt-ep-idle ep2

Section: Retransmission and Keepalive

 An unacknowledged QoS 1 PUBLISH past its deadline goes again with the
 DUP flag set (bit 3 of the fixed header, section 3.3.1.1), which tells
 the broker this is a redelivery and not a new message. After
 MAX_RETRANSMIT attempts the session is failed: a client that retries
 forever cannot tell a slow broker from a gone one.

  mqtt-ep-tick : MqttEp, Integer -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-tick (ep) (now) =
    if ep.me-acked then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if now < ep.me-pending-deadline then mqtt-ep-idle ep
    else if ep.me-retries >= mqtt-max-retransmit then mqtt-ep-idle (__record-set ep "me-state" mqtt-st-failed)
    else let again = if ep.me-phase == mqtt-ph-pubcomp then ep.me-pending-packet else mqtt-set-dup (ep.me-pending-packet)
    in let ep2 = __record-set (__record-set ep "me-retries" ((ep.me-retries) + 1)) "me-pending-deadline" (now + mqtt-ack-timeout)
    in MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep2, ms-out = [again], ms-consumed = 0 }

  mqtt-set-dup : List Integer -> List Integer
  mqtt-set-dup (pkt) =
    if list-length pkt == 0 then pkt
    else list-set-at (mqtt-slice pkt 0 (list-length pkt)) 0 (bit-or (list-at pkt 0) 8)

  mqtt-ep-ping : MqttEp -> MqttEpStep
  mqtt-ep-ping (ep) = MqttEpStep { ms-ep = ep, ms-out = [mqtt-build-pingreq], ms-consumed = 0 }

Section: Accessors

  mqtt-ep-state : MqttEp -> Integer
  mqtt-ep-state (ep) = ep.me-state

  mqtt-ep-ready : MqttEp -> Boolean
  mqtt-ep-ready (ep) = ep.me-state == mqtt-st-ready

  mqtt-ep-failed : MqttEp -> Boolean
  mqtt-ep-failed (ep) = ep.me-state == mqtt-st-failed

  mqtt-ep-connack : MqttEp -> Integer
  mqtt-ep-connack (ep) = ep.me-connack

  mqtt-ep-suback : MqttEp -> Integer
  mqtt-ep-suback (ep) = ep.me-suback

  mqtt-ep-published : MqttEp -> Boolean
  mqtt-ep-published (ep) = ep.me-acked

  mqtt-ep-retries : MqttEp -> Integer
  mqtt-ep-retries (ep) = ep.me-retries

  mqtt-ep-in-count : MqttEp -> Integer
  mqtt-ep-in-count (ep) = ep.me-in-count

  mqtt-ep-in-topic : MqttEp -> List Integer
  mqtt-ep-in-topic (ep) = ep.me-in-topic

  mqtt-ep-in-payload : MqttEp -> List Integer
  mqtt-ep-in-payload (ep) = ep.me-in-payload
